  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}+32x-29$ by completing the square.\n\n$-8{x}^{2}+32x-29=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{\u23df}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
